+| **✗ Violation: 2 new problems** | functional | medium | feature, behaviour change intended | [`TicketingApplication#holdPolicy`](../../examples/apps/boxoffice/ticketing-service/src/main/resources/application.yaml) | [B12: Boundary contracts](../../lib/README.md) |
+
+> Production wants a hold to be able to take a whole row, so a profile document for `prod` is added to the ticketing service's `application.yaml` with `boxoffice.hold.max-seat: 12`. The key the code reads is `max-seats`. Spring Boot ignores a key nothing binds, so production keeps the default of 8, and no test runs with the `prod` profile. The module's boundary contract lists each configuration key with the value each profile gives it and who reads it; the regenerated contract has a new line for the mistyped key, with a `!` under it that names the nearest known key.
+
+Tags: [spring-boot](../README.md#tag-spring-boot), [configuration-drift](../README.md#tag-configuration-drift), [contract-diff-review](../README.md#tag-contract-diff-review)
+
+## The change
+
+Let a hold in production take up to 12 seats instead of 8, so that a group can hold a whole row. The limit is a configuration key, `boxoffice.hold.max-seats`, read by the `HoldPolicy` bean with a default of 8, so the change is a profile document appended to `application.yaml`, activated by `spring.config.activate.on-profile: prod`. The key is written as `max-seat`.
+
+**Before** ([`application.yaml`](../../examples/apps/boxoffice/ticketing-service/src/main/resources/application.yaml), line 32):
+
+```java
+  sweep-interval: 1s
+outbox:
+  poll-interval: 100ms
+```
+
+**After:**
+
+```java
+    sweep-interval: 1s
+  outbox:
+    poll-interval: 100ms
+
+---
+# Production: a hold may take a whole row.
+spring:
+  config:
+    activate:
+      on-profile: prod
+boxoffice:
+  hold:
+    max-seat: 12
+```
